    <PackageReleaseNotes>v1.4.6: Fix for a group-bookkeeping race that could permanently orphan cache entries. `RemoveGroup` detached the whole group from the internal group map and then swept a snapshot of its keys, while an add registered its key in the group *before* writing the value to `MemoryCache`. A populate that straddled a concurrent `RemoveGroup` therefore landed its value in `MemoryCache` after the sweep had already passed, registered in a group object no longer reachable from the group map. The entry stayed live but invisible: no later `RemoveGroup` could evict it, and it served stale reads until its own TTL expired. Multi-user apps that invalidate a group on every write (while other requests populate the same group) hit this constantly — a measured 35% of entries were orphaned under a 4-thread invalidation load. The fix makes removal and addition agree on one source of truth: `RemoveGroup` now drains the group in place instead of detaching it, bumping a per-group sweep generation first; populates sample that generation before they run and roll their own entry back if it moved, so a concurrent invalidation always wins — even one issued while the populate was still reading its source data; single-key removal drops the value before the registration, so a sweep can never skip a key whose value is still live; and the background-refresh write-back is guarded the same way, so a refresh can no longer resurrect the persistent file a concurrent `RemoveGroup` just deleted. Five new regression tests (`GroupRemovalRaceTests`) reproduce the races deterministically — the concurrent-populate one orphaned 1,421 of 4,000 entries before the fix and zero after. 100% backward compatible; no public API changes.

v1.4.5: Fix for background auto-refresh on entries created via `Cache.GetAsync`. Since `GetAsync` shipped in 1.4.0, the `refresh` interval has been silently ignored for async entries: `CreateAndStoreCacheItemAsync` never stored the populate delegate on the `CacheItem&lt;T&gt;` (the sync path did), so `StartBackgroundRefresh` and `SetupRefreshTimer` both early-returned because the stored populate was null. Net effect: async entries stayed at their initial value until they expired, even when callers explicitly passed a refresh interval — both the periodic timer and the on-access refresh probe were dead-on-arrival. The fix adds a second populate slot on `CacheItem&lt;T&gt;` (`PopulateMethodCacheAsync`) for the async delegate, wires `CreateAndStoreCacheItemAsync` to populate it on both fresh and persistent-restore paths, and teaches `StartBackgroundRefresh` / `SetupRefreshTimer` to act on either populate variant. Async refreshes are awaited inside the background `Task.Run` so we never sync-over-async. Two new regression tests in `NewApiTests` cover the on-access and timer-driven refresh paths for `GetAsync`. 100% backward compatible.

v1.4.4: Fix for an async single-flight race in `Cache.GetAsync`. The in-flight dictionary previously stored bare `Task&lt;object&gt;` values and relied on `ConcurrentDictionary.GetOrAdd` to dedup concurrent populates. `GetOrAdd`'s factory may be invoked multiple times under contention (documented behavior), so the populate delegate could run more than once when several threads raced into a cold key — even though only one resulting Task was ultimately retained. Wrapping the in-flight value in `Lazy&lt;Task&lt;object&gt;&gt;` with `LazyThreadSafetyMode.ExecutionAndPublication` ensures the populate delegate runs exactly once across all racing callers. A new true-parallel unit test (`GetAsync_TrueConcurrent_PopulatesExactlyOnce`) reproduces the previous race deterministically. Sync path was already race-safe via `Lazy&lt;object&gt;` and is unchanged. 100% backward compatible.

v1.4.3: Eviction-lifecycle logging. Cache entries now emit a Debug log when they expire naturally ("Cache entry expired: {CacheKey} in group {GroupName}") and an Information log when evicted under memory pressure ("Cache entry evicted under memory pressure: ..."). Caller-initiated removals (Cache.Remove / RemoveGroup) are still logged upstream and intentionally suppressed at the eviction hook to avoid duplicate messages. This closes the lifecycle-visibility gap for persistent-cache consumers who need to audit whether their TTLs actually trigger. 100% backward compatible; opt-in via your existing LogLevel configuration (no new APIs).

v1.4.2: Release notes metadata fix; same code as v1.4.1.

v1.4.1: Critical fix for a deadlock introduced in 1.4.0. Synchronous Cache.Get called with a Task-returning populate (`await Cache.Get(..., async () =&gt; ...)`) could hang under single-threaded SynchronizationContexts (Blazor Server, WPF, WinForms) because the new eager size-estimator walked Task&lt;T&gt;.Result via JsonSerializer. Both RecomputeEstimatedSize and EstimateObjectSize now short-circuit when the value is a Task. Added a one-time Warning diagnostic (deduped per call site) that points sync-over-async callers at Cache.GetAsync. 100% backward compatible; recommended upgrade for all 1.4.0 users.</PackageReleaseNotes>
